---
import data from "../data/site-data.json";
import Figure from "./Figure.astro";
import { gh, int, num, pct } from "./format";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const cal = Object.fromEntries(data.nlsy.calibration.map((c) => [c.range_pct, c]));
const h = data.nsfg.headline.all;
const prim = data.m10.primary.find((p) => p.role === "primary")!;
const f2040 = data.m11.forecast[data.m11.forecast.length - 1];
const obs = data.m11.observed as [number, number][];
const last = obs[obs.length - 1];
const rows = data.final.populationMap as Record<string, string>[];
---
<section id="overview" aria-labelledby="q" tabindex="-1">
  <div class="stage-track" id="stage-overview">
    <div class="stage">
      <canvas class="field" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <div class="wrap grid hero">
        <div class="hero-text" id="hero-text">
          <span class="label kicker">An independent demographic inquiry</span>
          <h2 id="q">Will Generation&nbsp;Z have the families they say they want?</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Most young Americans say they want children, while U.S. fertility has fallen to about {num(last[1], 1)} births per woman.
            Together, the two facts invite a confident story: a generation that will fall short of its own plans. This project
            tested whether the evidence can support that story, with four studies built on seven official data sources.
          </p>
          <p class="answer"><strong>The short answer: the existing data cannot establish it.</strong> What they do show is narrower, and it is set out below, study by study.</p>
          <div class="byline">
            <span class="label">Sean Nathaniel M.</span>
            <span class="label">Frozen November 2026</span>
            <span class="label">Not peer-reviewed</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-figure">
          <div id="hero-chart" class="chart">
            <noscript><img class="fallback-img" src={`${base}figures/m11_tfr_sources.png`} alt="U.S. period total fertility rate, 1933 to 2024, from the Human Fertility Database, with NCHS values and the Census assumption." /></noscript>
          </div>
          <p class="hero-cap">
            <b>Fig. 0</b> U.S. period total fertility rate, 1953–2024. Each square is 0.1 births per woman, rounded; the line is exact.
            Source: Human Fertility Database (CC BY 4.0).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-cue label" aria-hidden="true"><i></i>Scroll</div>
      <div class="crowd-statement wrap" id="crowd-statement">
        <p class="statement">Behind every data point is a person, with a different timeline, different circumstances and different choices.</p>
        <p class="note">
          Surveys record what people say. Birth records count what happened. National rates summarize millions of lives at once.
          This project keeps those kinds of evidence apart, because they describe different people at different times.
        </p>
        <span class="label metaphor">Visual metaphor. The figures are illustrative; no square stands for a survey participant.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="studies wrap" id="studies">
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="c-1-4"><span class="label" style="color:var(--oxblood)">The investigation</span></div>
      <div class="c-4-13">
        <h2 class="ch-title" style="max-width:24ch">Four studies, four different kinds of evidence</h2>
        <p class="standfirst">
          Each study answers a narrower question that the headline question depends on. They observe different people, at
          different ages, in different years. Their numbers are therefore never added to or subtracted from one another.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ol class="study-list">
      <li>
        <span class="label" style="color:var(--oxblood)">01 Expectations</span>
        <a class="q" href="#expectations">Do stated chances of having a baby predict births?</a>
        <span class="who">NLSY97 · women born 1980–84 · followed 2009–2023 · not Generation Z</span>
        <span class="finding">Informative, but <b>far too extreme</b>: {pct(cal["0"].observed_pct / 100)} of women who said 0% had a birth within five years; {pct(cal["100"].observed_pct / 100)} of those who said 100% did.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="label" style="color:var(--oxblood)">02 Intentions</span>
        <a class="q" href="#intentions">How many young adults want, and intend, to have children?</a>
        <span class="who">NSFG 2022–23 · U.S. adults aged about 18–27 · one interview</span>
        <span class="finding"><b>{pct(h.wants.est)}</b> wanted a child and <b>{pct(h.intends.est)}</b> intended one. Demographic models could not identify who wants a child but does not intend one.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="label" style="color:var(--oxblood)">03 Housing</span>
        <a class="q" href="#housing">Do states where rent burden rises see fewer births?</a>
        <span class="who">ACS and CDC WONDER · 50 states and DC · 2014–2024</span>
        <span class="finding"><b>Not robustly</b>: {num(prim.est, 2)} births per 1,000 women aged 20–24 per 10 points of burden, interval {num(prim.lo, 2)} to {num(prim.hi, 2)}.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="label" style="color:var(--oxblood)">04 Forecast</span>
        <a class="q" href="#forecast">Where is U.S. fertility heading by 2040?</a>
        <span class="who">Human Fertility Database · United States · 1933–2024</span>
        <span class="finding"><b>No method beat &ldquo;no change&rdquo;.</b> The 2040 forecast is {num(f2040.f, 3)}, with a range from past errors that failed its calibration check.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="c-1-13">
        <Figure
          num="0.1"
          anchor="fig-coverage"
          title="Who each study observes"
          sub="Birth cohort by calendar year. Solid areas are observed; hatching is projected. Dashed diagonals mark ages 20, 30 and 45."
          chart="evidence"
          fallback="final_evidence_map.png"
          fallbackAlt="Calendar-year by birth-cohort map showing that no chapter follows Generation Z from stated desire to completed fertility."
          minH={360}
        >
          <p class="wide"><span class="k">Why it matters</span>No source follows Generation Z from stated desire to completed family size. A desired-versus-achieved gap would need both, for the same cohorts, so none is computed.</p>
          <p><span class="k">Source</span><a href={gh("data/public/final/final_population_map.csv")}>final_population_map.csv</a>, from the frozen synthesis.</p>
          <p class="cannot"><span class="k">Cannot show</span>Sample sizes or data quality. It is a map of coverage only.</p>
          <details class="data-table">
            <summary>Data table</summary>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table class="data">
                <thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Source</th><th>Years</th><th>Birth cohorts</th><th>Generation Z overlap</th><th>Inference</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {rows.map((r) => (
                    <tr><td>{r.chapter}</td><td>{r.data_source}</td><td>{r.observation_years}</td><td>{r.birth_cohorts}</td><td>{r.genz_overlap}</td><td>{r.inference}</td></tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </details>
        </Figure>
        <p class="statement" style="max-width:34ch;margin-top:var(--s-64)">
          That is why the project reports no &ldquo;intention gap&rdquo; as a number. <span class="hl">The name describes the question, not a measurement.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
